  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe point $P$ lies on the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{3}{7},$ use the Pythagorean Identity to find $sin\u2061\\theta .$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=sin\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{3}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ sin\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin\u2061\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}$",
